<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul Gauguin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1903">1903</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul Gauguin was a French Post-Impressionist painter, sculptor and printmaker. Leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1891, he spent most of his remaining years in Tahiti and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquesas-islands">Marquesas Islands</a>, where he painted the works, bold in colour and simplified in form, that shaped European images of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. He died at Atuona on Hiva Oa, and his work influenced modern artists from the Fauves to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Picasso</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-gauguin.jpg" alt="Paul Gauguin" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis-Maurice Boutet de Monvel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul_Gauguin_1891.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the 1880s younger painters, Vincent van Gogh, <b>Paul Gauguin</b>, Georges Seurat and Henri de Toulouse-Lautrec, reacted against the Impressionists’ concern with reproducing the optical sensations of light and colour and turned to symbolic content and the expression of emotion.</blockquote><a href="/movement/impressionism">Impressionism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>After a quarrel with <b>Paul Gauguin</b> in Arles, Vincent van Gogh cut off part of his left ear.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-23">23 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The painter <b>Paul Gauguin</b> made its image famous in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-establishments-in-oceania">French Establishments in Oceania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Atuona. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1883" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Paul Gauguin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1883">What was here in 1883? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
